   "group": "This computer",
   "html": "<h2 id=\"why\">Why <a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#why\" aria-label=\"Link to Why\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Your agents and jobs run on a laptop, a desktop, a home server and in CI. When a card says &quot;Tests\nfailing&quot;, you want to know which machine and which agent account it came from, and you want to revoke\none computer without touching the others. Relo keeps three names apart:</p>\n<div class=\"docs-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Tells apart</th>\n<th>Set by</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Login</strong> (client name)</td>\n<td>each computer or job signed in to your server</td>\n<td><code>relo auth login --client-name</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Computer</strong></td>\n<td>where a card ran</td>\n<td>automatic; <code>RELO_COMPUTER</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Agent account</strong></td>\n<td>which Claude or Codex account ran it (work, personal)</td>\n<td><code>relo account NAME</code>; <code>RELO_ACCOUNT</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>profile</strong> is a second sign-in on the same computer, for a job that should have its own login.</p>\n<h2 id=\"one-login-per-computer\">One login per computer <a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#one-login-per-computer\" aria-label=\"Link to One login per computer\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Sign in once on each computer. The login is named after the computer by default; give it a clearer name\nif you like:</p>\n<figure class=\"docs-code\" data-language=\"sh\"><button class=\"docs-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"relo auth login --api-url https://relo.example.com --client-name &quot;Studio desktop&quot;\" hidden>Copy</button><pre><code>relo auth login --api-url https://relo.example.com --client-name &quot;Studio desktop&quot;</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Keep names unique per machine: a login is replaced when the same name signs in again. Each login appears\nin the Dashboard, where you can revoke it on its own. <code>relo auth status</code> shows who is signed in here.\nEvery flag: <a href=\"/docs/reference/cli/account\">account reference</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"profiles-for-separate-jobs\">Profiles for separate jobs <a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#profiles-for-separate-jobs\" aria-label=\"Link to Profiles for separate jobs\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An app or job that should be revocable on its own, or scoped to less, gets its own sign-in beside yours:</p>\n<figure class=\"docs-code\" data-language=\"sh\"><button class=\"docs-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"relo auth login --profile billing --client-name &quot;Billing app&quot;\" hidden>Copy</button><pre><code>relo auth login --profile billing --client-name &quot;Billing app&quot;</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Then pick it per command, or make it the active one:</p>\n<figure class=\"docs-code\" data-language=\"sh\"><button class=\"docs-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"RELO_PROFILE=billing relo ask &quot;Refund order 8812 (240 EUR)?&quot; --approval\nrelo auth switch billing\" hidden>Copy</button><pre><code>RELO_PROFILE=billing relo ask &quot;Refund order 8812 (240 EUR)?&quot; --approval\nrelo auth switch billing</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>relo auth status</code> shows the active profile. <code>RELO_TOKEN</code> and <code>RELO_API_URL</code> still win over any profile,\nwhich is how CI signs in (see <a href=\"/docs/guides/scripts#ci\">Scripts, cron and CI</a>). The one-time phone test\nis remembered per profile.</p>\n<h2 id=\"computer-names\">Computer names <a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#computer-names\" aria-label=\"Link to Computer names\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Each card shows the computer it ran on: the Mac's name, else the hostname. Override it when the hostname\nsays nothing, for example on a server or in a container:</p>\n<figure class=\"docs-code\" data-language=\"sh\"><button class=\"docs-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"export RELO_COMPUTER=&quot;Home server&quot;\" hidden>Copy</button><pre><code>export RELO_COMPUTER=&quot;Home server&quot;</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>--computer NAME</code> on a single command does the same.</p>\n<h2 id=\"agent-accounts\">Agent accounts <a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#agent-accounts\" aria-label=\"Link to Agent accounts\">#</a></h2>\n<p>If you run Claude Code or Codex under two accounts on one computer (a work and a personal one), the card\nsays which. Relo detects a custom config folder by its name (<code>~/.claude-work</code> becomes &quot;work&quot;) without\nreading anything inside it. Name an account yourself from a session that runs under it:</p>\n<figure class=\"docs-code\" data-language=\"sh\"><button class=\"docs-copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"relo account work\" hidden>Copy</button><pre><code>relo account work</code></pre></figure>\n<p>With no name it shows the current one; <code>--clear</code> removes the nickname. <code>RELO_ACCOUNT</code> sets it for every\ncommand in a shell, and <code>--account NAME</code> for one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it\">Check it <a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#check-it\" aria-label=\"Link to Check it\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>relo auth status</code> shows the server, the login and the profile.</li>\n<li>Start a card from each computer: its details show the computer and the account.</li>\n<li>In the Dashboard, each login is listed by its client name, ready to revoke.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All variables: <a href=\"/docs/reference/environment\">environment reference</a>. How names are picked, in order:\n<code>relo help identity</code>.</p>\n",
